<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1000 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Song of the Sea</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שִׁירַת הַיָּם</bdi> / <bdi lang="hbo-Latn">Shirat HaYam</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song in the Bible that the Israelites sang after crossing the sea, free at last after years of slavery in Egypt.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; ascribed in Exodus to Moses and the Israelites, with Miriam</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>ancient Israelites; Jewish and Christian worshippers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Biblical Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>ancient Israel (not known exactly)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>proposed dates range from the 13th to the 5th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words anonymous (Exodus 15:1–18))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea</a>, also known by its opening words as Az Yashir Moshe and by a later line as Mi Chamocha, is a poem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a> (15:1–18). In the biblical story the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> sang it after crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> in safety, celebrating their freedom after generations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and oppression in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; its first two verses are repeated in verses 20 and 21, where Miriam and other Israelite women sing them. Christians, following Revelation 15:3, call it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>.</p><p>The poem is written in a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> much older than the rest of Exodus. Some scholars consider it the oldest surviving text describing the Exodus, from before the Israelite monarchy; others think it was written in an archaic style on purpose, a known literary device. Proposed dates therefore range from the 13th to the 5th century BCE. Joshua Berman, comparing the texts closely, argues that the Exodus sea account appropriates the Poem of Pentaur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>'s account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>, possibly with satirical intent as part of an ideological contest with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>.</p><p>The song is one of only two sections of the Torah scroll written in a layout other than plain columns. The Ashkar-Gilson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscript</a>, a fragment of a 7th-century Torah scroll, already shows the &quot;brickwork&quot; pattern, which some scholars take as evidence that the Masoretes copied earlier manuscripts faithfully; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a> do not use it. In cantillation the passage has its own festive tune, not bound to the ordinary trope marks.</p><p>The poem is recited daily in the morning prayers (Shaharit) of Jewish prayer books, and the Shabbat on which its Torah portion, Beshalach, is read is called Shabbat Shirah, the Sabbath of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>. Most Jewish communities sing a related Ladino poem, the Ketubá del Seten Diya de Pesah, on the seventh day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>, the day tradition gives for the drowning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>'s army and the singing of the song. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the poem is the first ode of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> canon and is used in the Catholic Easter Vigil.</p><p>Composers set the song from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> on, among them Étienne Moulinié, Fernando de las Infantas and Ascanio Trombetti, Handel in Part Three of his oratorio Israel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> (1739) and Schubert in Mirjams Siegesgesang. Lines and paraphrases from it appear in &quot;When You Believe&quot; from the 1998 film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a> of Egypt, and in the African-American gospel song &quot;O Mary Don't You Weep&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of thanksgiving and triumph that praises God for bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> safely through the sea and for the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a>'s army. Its mood is the joy of a people newly freed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Biblical Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button></div><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Biblical Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אָ֣ז יָשִֽׁיר־מֹשֶׁה֩ וּבְנֵ֨י יִשְׂרָאֵ֜ל אֶת־הַשִּׁירָ֤ה הַזֹּאת֙ לַֽיהֹוָ֔ה וַיֹּאמְר֖וּ לֵאמֹ֑ר אָשִׁ֤ירָה לַֽיהֹוָה֙ כִּֽי־גָאֹ֣ה גָּאָ֔ה ס֥וּס וְרֹכְב֖וֹ רָמָ֥ה בַיָּֽם׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">az yashir-Moshe uvenei Yisra'el et-hashirah hazzot laYHWH vayyomeru lemor ashirah laYHWH ki-ga'oh ga'ah sus verokhvo ramah vayyam.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עׇזִּ֤י וְזִמְרָת֙ יָ֔הּ וַֽיְהִי־לִ֖י לִֽישׁוּעָ֑ה זֶ֤ה אֵלִי֙ וְאַנְוֵ֔הוּ אֱלֹהֵ֥י אָבִ֖י וַאֲרֹמְמֶֽנְהוּ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">'ozzi vezimrat yah vayhi-li lishu'ah zeh eli ve'anvehu elohei avi va'aromemenhu.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יְהֹוָ֖ה אִ֣ישׁ מִלְחָמָ֑ה יְהֹוָ֖ה שְׁמֽוֹ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">YHWH ish milkhamah YHWH shemo.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מַרְכְּבֹ֥ת פַּרְעֹ֛ה וְחֵיל֖וֹ יָרָ֣ה בַיָּ֑ם וּמִבְחַ֥ר שָֽׁלִשָׁ֖יו טֻבְּע֥וּ בְיַם־סֽוּף׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">markevot Par'oh vekheilo yarah vayyam umivkhar shalishav tubbe'u veyam-suf.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תְּהֹמֹ֖ת יְכַסְיֻ֑מוּ יָרְד֥וּ בִמְצוֹלֹ֖ת כְּמוֹ־אָֽבֶן׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">tehomot yekhasyumu yaredu vimtsolot kemo-aven.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יְמִֽינְךָ֣ יְהֹוָ֔ה נֶאְדָּרִ֖י בַּכֹּ֑חַ יְמִֽינְךָ֥ יְהֹוָ֖ה תִּרְעַ֥ץ אוֹיֵֽב׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">yeminekha YHWH ne'dari bakkoakh yeminekha YHWH tir'ats oyev.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">וּבְרֹ֥ב גְּאוֹנְךָ֖ תַּהֲרֹ֣ס קָמֶ֑יךָ תְּשַׁלַּח֙ חֲרֹ֣נְךָ֔ יֹאכְלֵ֖מוֹ כַּקַּֽשׁ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">uverov ge'onekha taharos kamekha teshallakh kharonekha yokhelemo kakkash.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">וּבְר֤וּחַ אַפֶּ֙יךָ֙ נֶ֣עֶרְמוּ מַ֔יִם נִצְּב֥וּ כְמוֹ־נֵ֖ד נֹזְלִ֑ים קָֽפְא֥וּ תְהֹמֹ֖ת בְּלֶב־יָֽם׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">uveruakh appekha ne'ermu mayim nitsevu khemo-ned nozelim kafe'u tehomot belev-yam.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אָמַ֥ר אוֹיֵ֛ב אֶרְדֹּ֥ף אַשִּׂ֖יג אֲחַלֵּ֣ק שָׁלָ֑ל תִּמְלָאֵ֣מוֹ נַפְשִׁ֔י אָרִ֣יק חַרְבִּ֔י תּוֹרִישֵׁ֖מוֹ יָדִֽי׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">amar oyev erdof assig akhallek shalal timla'emo nafshi arik kharbi torishemo yadi.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">נָשַׁ֥פְתָּ בְרוּחֲךָ֖ כִּסָּ֣מוֹ יָ֑ם צָֽלְלוּ֙ כַּֽעוֹפֶ֔רֶת בְּמַ֖יִם אַדִּירִֽים׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">nashafta veruakhakha kissamo yam tsalelu ka'oferet bemayim addirim.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מִֽי־כָמֹ֤כָה בָּֽאֵלִם֙ יְהֹוָ֔ה מִ֥י כָּמֹ֖כָה נֶאְדָּ֣ר בַּקֹּ֑דֶשׁ נוֹרָ֥א תְהִלֹּ֖ת עֹ֥שֵׂה פֶֽלֶא׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">mi-khamokhah ba'elim YHWH mi kamokhah ne'dar bakkodesh nora tehillot 'oseh fele.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">נָטִ֙יתָ֙ יְמִ֣ינְךָ֔ תִּבְלָעֵ֖מוֹ אָֽרֶץ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">natita yeminekha tivla'emo arets.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">נָחִ֥יתָ בְחַסְדְּךָ֖ עַם־ז֣וּ גָּאָ֑לְתָּ נֵהַ֥לְתָּ בְעׇזְּךָ֖ אֶל־נְוֵ֥ה קׇדְשֶֽׁךָ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">nakhita vekhasdekha 'am-zu ga'alta nehalta ve'ozzekha el-neveh kodshekha.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שָֽׁמְע֥וּ עַמִּ֖ים יִרְגָּז֑וּן חִ֣יל אָחַ֔ז יֹשְׁבֵ֖י פְּלָֽשֶׁת׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">shame'u 'ammim yirgazun khil akhaz yoshevei Pelashet.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אָ֤ז נִבְהֲלוּ֙ אַלּוּפֵ֣י אֱד֔וֹם אֵילֵ֣י מוֹאָ֔ב יֹֽאחֲזֵ֖מוֹ רָ֑עַד נָמֹ֕גוּ כֹּ֖ל יֹשְׁבֵ֥י כְנָֽעַן׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">az nivhalu allufei Edom eilei Mo'av yokhazemo ra'ad namogu kol yoshevei Khena'an.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תִּפֹּ֨ל עֲלֵיהֶ֤ם אֵימָ֙תָה֙ וָפַ֔חַד בִּגְדֹ֥ל זְרוֹעֲךָ֖ יִדְּמ֣וּ כָּאָ֑בֶן עַד־יַעֲבֹ֤ר עַמְּךָ֙ יְהֹוָ֔ה עַֽד־יַעֲבֹ֖ר עַם־ז֥וּ קָנִֽיתָ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">tippol 'aleihem eimatah vafakhad bigdol zero'akha yiddemu ka'aven 'ad-ya'avor 'ammekha YHWH 'ad-ya'avor 'am-zu kanita.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תְּבִאֵ֗מוֹ וְתִטָּעֵ֙מוֹ֙ בְּהַ֣ר נַחֲלָֽתְךָ֔ מָכ֧וֹן לְשִׁבְתְּךָ֛ פָּעַ֖לְתָּ יְהֹוָ֑ה מִקְּדָ֕שׁ אֲדֹנָ֖י כּוֹנְנ֥וּ יָדֶֽיךָ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">tevi'emo vetitta'emo behar nakhalatekha makhon leshivtekha pa'alta YHWH mikkedash Adonai konenu yadekha.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יְהֹוָ֥ה ׀ יִמְלֹ֖ךְ לְעֹלָ֥ם וָעֶֽד׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">YHWH yimlokh le'olam va'ed.</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then sang Moses and the children of Israel this song unto the LORD, and spake, saying, I will sing unto the LORD, for he hath triumphed gloriously: the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The LORD is my strength and song, and he is become my salvation: he is my God, and I will prepare him an habitation; my father's God, and I will exalt him.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The LORD is a man of war: the LORD is his name.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Pharaoh's chariots and his host hath he cast into the sea: his chosen captains also are drowned in the Red sea.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The depths have covered them: they sank into the bottom as a stone.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thy right hand, O LORD, is become glorious in power: thy right hand, O LORD, hath dashed in pieces the enemy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And in the greatness of thine excellency thou hast overthrown them that rose up against thee: thou sentest forth thy wrath, which consumed them as stubble.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And with the blast of thy nostrils the waters were gathered together, the floods stood upright as an heap, and the depths were congealed in the heart of the sea.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The enemy said, I will pursue, I will overtake, I will divide the spoil; my lust shall be satisfied upon them; I will draw my sword, my hand shall destroy them.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou didst blow with thy wind, the sea covered them: they sank as lead in the mighty waters.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who is like unto thee, O LORD, among the gods? who is like thee, glorious in holiness, fearful in praises, doing wonders?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou stretchedst out thy right hand, the earth swallowed them.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou in thy mercy hast led forth the people which thou hast redeemed: thou hast guided them in thy strength unto thy holy habitation.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The people shall hear, and be afraid: sorrow shall take hold on the inhabitants of Palestina.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then the dukes of Edom shall be amazed; the mighty men of Moab, trembling shall take hold upon them; all the inhabitants of Canaan shall melt away.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fear and dread shall fall upon them; by the greatness of thine arm they shall be as still as a stone; till thy people pass over, O LORD, till the people pass over, which thou hast purchased.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou shalt bring them in, and plant them in the mountain of thine inheritance, in the place, O LORD, which thou hast made for thee to dwell in, in the Sanctuary, O LORD, which thy hands have established.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The LORD shall reign for ever and ever.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Exodus 15:1–18, Hebrew Bible (Masoretic text). Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%A9%D7%9E%D7%95%D7%AA_%D7%98%D7%95" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose).</p><p>English: King James Version (1611). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)%2FExodus%2FChapter_15" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>SHAPE: orig is ONE stanza of 18 lines (one per verse, Exodus 15:1-18); the KJV 'en' in texts/song-of-the-sea.json is 18 stanzas of one line each. Same verses, different nesting: one of the two needs reshaping before they are paired.</li><li>KJV import slip, verse 8: 'the floods floods stood upright as an heap' has 'floods' twice (KJV: 'the floods stood upright'). Fix in the import.</li><li>line 1.10: the source has a stray U+FB1E (Hebrew point judeo-spanish varika) in 'tsalelu' (צָֽלְﬞלוּ), a typographic mark of the Wikisource edition; ignored in rom, worth cleaning in orig.</li><li>line 1.1 begins with the narrative frame ('Then Moses and the children of Israel sang this song to YHWH and said:'), not part of the song itself.</li><li>The divine name is romanized as written, YHWH (read Adonai), and YAH as yah (line 1.2); line 1.17 writes Adonai itself (אדני) and is romanized so. The editor may prefer 'Adonai' for YHWH throughout.</li><li>Cantillation marks and the paseq are not romanized; qamats katan read as o (kodshekha, 'ozzekha). Initial aleph unmarked, other aleph and all ayin as '; dagesh forte doubled except in sh, ts, kh; maqqef as a hyphen.</li></ul></details></section>
